<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franciscans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1209">1209</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Order of Friars Minor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Franciscans are the Catholic religious orders that follow the rule of Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, approved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> between 1209 and 1223, chief among them the Order of Friars Minor. Vowed to poverty and preaching, the friars spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and became leading missionaries in the Spanish and Portuguese empires. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> they founded missions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, often building churches and towns among indigenous peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franciscans.jpg" alt="Franciscans" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Piotr Jaworski, PioM · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FrancescoCoA_PioM.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Monasteries became centres of prayer, learning, farming and charity; in the West they preserved classical texts and founded schools, and later religious orders, such as the <b>Franciscans</b> and Dominicans in the 13th century and the Jesuits in the 16th, preached, taught and led missions across the world.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1598 Juan de Oñate led settlers, soldiers and <b>Franciscans</b> north and founded the colony of New Mexico; after Acoma’s warriors killed a party of his soldiers, his forces stormed the mesa in January 1599 and killed several hundred of its people.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The Theatines were founded in 1524 by Gaetano and Cardinal Gian Caraffa; the Capuchins, an offshoot of the <b>Franciscans</b> proposed by Matteo de Bascio in 1526, were noted for preaching and care of the poor and sick; the Ursulines were the first order of women devoted to educating…</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote>New orders spread fast: the Cistercians from 1098, and in the 13th century the friars, <b>Franciscans</b> and Dominicans, who preached in the towns and lived by begging.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Franciscans</b> built a great convent on a pyramid at Izamal in the 1550s.</blockquote><a href="/state/ah-kin-chel">Ah Kin Chel</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Franciscans</b> under Junípero Serra founded a mission at San Diego that year, and Monterey, founded in 1770, later became the capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/alta-california">Alta California</a></li></ul></section>
